  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 257 PM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nBy Tuesday morning, there's is an area of high \npressure that begins to form over the Great Lakes region as our \nformer low pressure system enters the Northern plains causing a \ntraffic jam over the east coast. This becomes our problem as the \nridge builds over us it blocks the weak low pressure system off the \nwest coast. Multiple shortwaves will be pushing through the \nIntermountain west with the first one starting late Tuesday evening \nthrough Wednesday morning for possible light rain showers. With the \nlater timing off this wave its going to be hard to maintain higher \nenergy levels needed for severe thunderstorms as the sun angle is \nslowly starting to decline. Wednesday afternoon through Thursday \nevening looks pretty dry as the Intermountain maintains southwest \nflow on the front edge of the ridge advecting in the dry airmass of \nthe desert southwest. Thursday night into Friday morning another Low \npressure system is progged to push into the Pacific Northwest to \ncause some light rain showers to possibly occur Friday. Daily high \ntemperatures will fluctuate as 700mb temperatures bounce between 6 \nand 12 degrees C. So on our dry days expect temperatures to range \nbetween the 70's and low 80's to 60/70's on our rainy days. \nOvernight temperatures will remain relatively stable with the \novernight lows to remain in the 40's to with some areas reaching the \nlow 50's. This weekend is starting to trend warmer and drier with \nthe global models wanting to amplify the ridging pattern over us \nshifting the potential Low pressure system from Friday further north \ninto the Canadian Provinces.",
